WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.740
The year was 2006 and the Winter Olympics
was happening in Turin, Italy.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.349
A lot of pressure was on the 
American skater, Apolo Ohno

00:00:08.349 --> 00:00:11.655
for the 500 meter finals in 
short track speed skating.

00:00:11.655 --> 00:00:15.455
"But make no mistake about this and he said it:
I want to win."

00:00:15.455 --> 00:00:18.617
And it was like something I was deeply 
in pursuit of 

00:00:18.617 --> 00:00:22.011
having a perfect strategic race 
that would go my way.

00:00:22.011 --> 00:00:26.460
As he entered the final lap, he maintained
a strong lead.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:30.519
You're almost like a little bit out of control
because you want it so badly.

00:00:30.519 --> 00:00:35.233
But you're also maintaining composure to protect
your lane and your position.

00:00:35.233 --> 00:00:37.115
And he pulled it off.

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:45.080
After that gold medal, Apolo Ohno was launched
into mainstream fame.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:46.120
He was in ads.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:47.796
He was on a Wheaties box.

00:00:47.796 --> 00:00:50.940
His bandana and facial hair became iconic.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:54.570
He competed on Dancing with the Stars... and
won.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.098
It was an awesome time.

00:00:56.098 --> 00:01:01.154
But as an athlete, those 41.93 seconds 
meant something else, too.

00:01:01.154 --> 00:01:05.200
And not because he was necessarily the fastest
skater going into the race

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.878
but the smartest and the luckiest.

00:01:07.878 --> 00:01:11.150
Every single half a lap had to have occurred
in that way

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:13.348
in order for me to have had the perfect race.

00:01:13.348 --> 00:01:15.644
Here’s how he did it.

00:01:17.985 --> 00:01:22.448
A short track race like Apolo Ohno’s 
can be broken down into 4 main parts.

00:01:22.448 --> 00:01:25.850
The first thing that went right was the start.

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:30.191
In short track, 4 to 6 skaters all start 
on the same line.

00:01:30.191 --> 00:01:32.012
And take off in a free-for-all.

00:01:32.012 --> 00:01:35.330
Short track skaters don’t have designated
lanes during the race

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:38.490
unlike long track skating 
or running events.

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:40.806
Short track skaters aren’t racing the clock.

00:01:40.806 --> 00:01:44.054
They are only racing against each other.

00:01:44.054 --> 00:01:48.040
Which is why short track is all about the
way athletes interact:

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.670
jostling for the right position on the rink,
trying not to fall

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:55.162
and avoiding disqualifications 
from illegal contact.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:02.740
When skaters take off in the 500 meter event, 
they have one goal in mind:

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.824
getting to the first turn first.

00:02:05.824 --> 00:02:10.159
Research shows that whoever leads in the first
turn in the 500 meter event

00:02:10.159 --> 00:02:13.108
is far more likely to win the race.

00:02:13.108 --> 00:02:17.205
The person in starting position 1 has the
shortest path to the first turn.

00:02:17.205 --> 00:02:23.582
But skaters in other positions with faster
starts could box position 1 in from the outside.

00:02:23.582 --> 00:02:27.899
Like the second position skater here, at the
2010 Winter Olympics.

00:02:29.827 --> 00:02:33.146
Today, short track starting positions 
are merit-based

00:02:33.146 --> 00:02:37.633
that means the fastest skater from earlier
rounds gets the first position.

00:02:37.633 --> 00:02:40.340
But at the time of the 2006 Olympics

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:43.329
skater positions were computer-selected at
random

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:46.065
which would happen right before a race.

00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:47.709
I'm in the locker room.

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:49.240
I'm sharpening my skates.

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:50.848
I'm getting ready.

00:02:50.848 --> 00:02:53.520
I hear this scream in the hallway.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.000
And it's one of my coaches...

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.996
who told Apolo that he was selected 
for the first position.

00:02:58.996 --> 00:03:01.459
That stroke of luck and a fast start

00:03:01.459 --> 00:03:03.999
helped Apollo Ohno lead in that first turn

00:03:03.999 --> 00:03:05.586
and hold it the whole way.

00:03:05.586 --> 00:03:06.753
"He just gets the turn first."

00:03:06.753 --> 00:03:09.126
"This is exactly where Apolo wants to be..."

00:03:12.734 --> 00:03:17.714
A short track rink is an oval 
that measures 111.12 meters.

00:03:17.714 --> 00:03:21.650
This shape means that skaters are turning
nearly half the time.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:24.056
When they turn, it looks like this.

00:03:24.056 --> 00:03:26.540
It actually looks like it doesn't make sense.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:31.031
At the apex of a corner, skaters are turning
on an 8 meter radius.

00:03:31.031 --> 00:03:33.822
And can lean up to 22 degrees.

00:03:33.822 --> 00:03:38.018
So close to the ice, that their left gloved
hands graze it.

00:03:38.018 --> 00:03:40.139
One short track skater told me that

00:03:40.139 --> 00:03:45.650
at a speed of roughly 30 miles per hour, they
are pushing against a g-force equivalent to

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:48.280
2-3 times their body weight.

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:54.506
Like doing a really hard squat: hunched over,
at an angle, on one leg.

00:03:54.506 --> 00:03:58.910
Which is exactly how they train for turns,
like these videos that British short track

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:00.899
athletes posted online show.

00:04:00.899 --> 00:04:06.529
In the 2006 race, Apolo Ohno used his
strength to keep his turns really tight.

00:04:06.529 --> 00:04:09.379
Meaning I would hug the middle of the apex
of the corner

00:04:09.379 --> 00:04:10.930
all the way to the seventh block.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:14.398
So there actually was no position 
to pass on the inside.

00:04:14.398 --> 00:04:17.300
There are two common ways of executing a pass.

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:21.072
Both require using the forces of the turn
and the right timing.

00:04:21.072 --> 00:04:23.072
One is from the inside.

00:04:23.072 --> 00:04:27.722
The passing skater uses the draft of the skater
in front of them to pick up speed,

00:04:27.722 --> 00:04:31.900
then pulls wider on the turn, to slingshot
out of the turn

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:35.000
and overtake the skater in front on the inside.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.374
Like this pass from the 2014 Sochi Olympics.

00:04:40.608 --> 00:04:43.656
The other, trickier pass, is on the outside.

00:04:43.656 --> 00:04:48.640
The reason why it's so much more difficult
to pass on the outside is because you need

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:51.252
to have significantly more speed.

00:04:51.252 --> 00:04:53.470
An outside pass starts at the apex

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:57.350
using the speed coming out of the turn 
to sprint past the lead skater

00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:00.082
as you enter the next turn.

00:05:00.082 --> 00:05:04.780
Like this outside pass also at 
the 2014 Sochi Olympics.

00:05:07.428 --> 00:05:09.100
It's a confidence crusher.

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:12.170
If you pass someone and they know that you
passed them easily

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:16.417
you're signaling to them, "Oh, by the way,
the four years of training you just did?

00:05:16.417 --> 00:05:17.660
It just wasn't enough."

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:18.720
"You're just not fast enough."

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:21.306
"I'm sorry."

00:05:21.306 --> 00:05:25.840
When you’re in the lead, picking a track
pattern is how you keep from getting passed.

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:31.170
One common track pattern is the diamond which
defends against passes on the outside.

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:36.970
Or the peanut pattern, which defends against
inside passes.

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:41.424
The first three laps, Apolo Ohno did a mix
of patterns to keep anyone in the pack 

00:05:41.424 --> 00:05:43.490
from picking up speed.

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:47.878
"This is really a perfect position for Apolo,
he's taking it nice and easy..."

00:05:47.878 --> 00:05:52.270
So the idea was to kind of skate this track
pattern

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:57.892
that was a little bit confusing to them where
they would second guess making a move.

00:05:57.892 --> 00:06:00.547
It’s like the person walking in front of you 
in the mall 

00:06:00.547 --> 00:06:03.546
that keeps stepping in front of you, 
no matter how fast you go.

00:06:03.546 --> 00:06:06.924
You have to restart your speed again.

00:06:06.924 --> 00:06:10.152
On the last two laps, Apolo Ohno 
kicked up the speed...

00:06:10.152 --> 00:06:13.088
"And here he goes. He's just kicking into gear..."

00:06:13.088 --> 00:06:17.264
... and skated in a peanut pattern to prevent
an inside pass.

00:06:17.264 --> 00:06:20.933
I knew that the likelihood of someone 
passing me on the outside 

00:06:20.933 --> 00:06:23.670
in the 500 meters at the last two laps

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:26.788
if I'm going 100 percent all out,
was very low.

00:06:26.788 --> 00:06:29.640
And so with two laps remaining, 
it's exactly what I did.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:34.150
The last element of short track is the hardest
to prepare for.

00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:37.460
The best way to illustrate this is another
one of Apolo’s races

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:40.286
at the 2002 Olympics in Salt Lake City.

00:06:40.286 --> 00:06:43.738
This one is infamous in short track history.

00:06:43.738 --> 00:06:48.149
It was the 1,000 meter final and Apolo was skating against underdog 

00:06:48.149 --> 00:06:50.810
Australian skater, Steven Bradbury.

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:52.428
I was the favorite to win that race.

00:06:52.428 --> 00:06:56.882
Nobody considered Stephen to even be 
in the running to medal.

00:06:56.882 --> 00:07:00.788
As the skaters approached the last lap, 
Apolo was in the lead

00:07:00.788 --> 00:07:04.370
and Steven Bradbury was far behind the pack

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:09.319
until the last minute scramble, 
resulted in... this:

00:07:09.319 --> 00:07:11.840
"A total wipe out!"

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:13.641
"And Bradbury wins!"

00:07:13.641 --> 00:07:15.947
"Four skaters go down..."

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:20.350
Falls happen often in short track racing

00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:23.190
because of the fight for the best position
on the rink.

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:26.414
And sometimes they are beyond 
a skater’s control.

00:07:26.414 --> 00:07:31.582
Which means, like other sports, bad luck 
can undermine the greatest skill.

00:07:31.582 --> 00:07:35.694
It also means resilience is a virtue 
in short track.

00:07:35.694 --> 00:07:39.820
You now have this like unique ability to

00:07:39.820 --> 00:07:45.000
either react and respond in a way that will
give you a stewed bitterness 

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:46.706
for the rest of your life.

00:07:46.706 --> 00:07:47.850
Or you have to reset.

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:48.850
Couldn't control that.

00:07:48.850 --> 00:07:51.162
I showed up as prepared as possible.

00:07:51.162 --> 00:07:53.594
Life and the universe had 
a different plan for me.

00:07:53.594 --> 00:07:59.404
If you’re Apolo Ohno, that plan was that,
4 years later, he would get his perfect race.

00:07:59.404 --> 00:08:02.154
No falls, or blunders, or passes.

00:08:02.154 --> 00:08:03.968
Just a good clean win.

